# ── Benchmark recording outputs ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Per CLAUDE.md → Durability & backup: git is authoritative for SMALL TEXT
# (results.json, metadata.json, 0_README.md, notes.md, cmd.txt, pre/, post/, and the
# summary CSVs at the runs/ root — all COMMITTED). S3 is authoritative for the heavy
# write-once telemetry below, which must NOT enter git.
runs/*/raw/
runs/*/cmd.log
runs/sweep-logs/

# runs/.leg-state/ is deliberately NOT ignored — it is TRACKED.
#
# It holds run-leg.sh's per-step completion markers, and the earlier reasoning
# ("per-leg scratch state, recreated by the orchestrator, meaningless on another
# instance") was wrong in the one case that matters: the instance is rebuilt at least
# twice, and these markers are the only thing that stops a resumed leg from re-running
# every completed step. Ignored + not synced to S3 meant they died with the instance,
# so TEARDOWN-AND-REBUILD.md's "a rebuild mid-leg picks up where it stopped" was false
# — a rebuilt `run-leg.sh --leg $LEG` would silently redo hours of sweeps and create
# duplicate timestamped run dirs, polluting the aggregates rather than erroring.
#
# git carries them because CLAUDE.md's authority split already says git is
# authoritative for all small text; S3 was the alternative, but its never-delete
# archive semantics would resurrect a marker deleted on purpose to force a re-run.
# The dirty-tree concern is moot: teardown step 5 commits everything anyway, which is
# exactly the moment these need to be captured.

# Large per-workload telemetry CSVs — per-interval output balloons on long endurance
# cells (hundreds of MB each), exceeds GitHub per-file limits, and is regenerable from
# the raw output that is already excluded. Headline numbers live in the summary CSVs.
**/workload-*.csv

# Defensive: catch the same patterns at any depth, in case a subtree is ever added.
**/runs/*/raw/
**/runs/*/cmd.log
**/runs/sweep-logs/

